<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="salidroside biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6802" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: CPD-13354 Salidroside| is an important pharmaceutical compound isolated from Rhodiola species. These species are mainly found in alpine regions of Asia and Scandinavia. It is an adaptogen with medicinal properties such as anti-fatigue, anti-senescence and anti-radiation effects. Natural habitats are limiting for its continuous supply and the cultivation of this plant requires 4-5 years, therefore, alternate ways to produce salidroside have been explored using in vitro studies. |FRAME: CPD-13354 Salidroside| is a phenylethanoid glycoside and is derived from phenylalanine or |FRAME: TYR|. Over expression of PAL gene did not increase |FRAME: CPD-13354| production as shown in this study |CITS: [18426479 ]|. |FRAME: CPD-13354| production, using |FRAME: TAX-265354| hairy roots induced by Agrobacterium rhizogenes gave promising yields. The optimal concentration of the precursors; |FRAME: CPD3O-4151|, phenylalanine and |FRAME: TYR| along with elicitor concentration was established in this study |CITS: [17329834]|. |FRAME: CPD-13354 Salidroside| is mainly found in the roots. The glycosylation of |FRAME: CPD3O-4151| is the final step of the biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-13354|, the glycosyltransferases responsible for salidroside biosynthesis have been isolated |CITS: [21497865]| and |CITS: [17333022]|. The biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD3O-4151| was worked out latter; its precursor was thought to be |FRAME: TYRAMINE|, which is synthesized from |FRAME: TYR|. The isolation and over expression of a tyrosine decarboxylase resulted in the increase in |FRAME: CPD3O-4151| and |FRAME: CPD-13354| production, indicating its role in the biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-13354| |CITS: [21538102]|. Further, in this study |CITS: [15227984]| it has been shown that red light promotes |FRAME: CPD-13354| production but decreases the plant biomass.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="zhang" />
